-- EXPECT-ERROR: `Cap(IO.FileWrite)` used in module `BodyAnnCap` but `IO.FileWrite` is not declared in `needs`
-- `needs` coverage reaches inside function BODIES (2026-08-05).
--
-- Check 1 read function SIGNATURES. A type annotation written inside a body
-- is not part of any signature, so it escaped `needs` entirely and this
-- typechecked with exit 0 under `--cap-strict`.
--
-- A LAMBDA PARAMETER is the sharpest witness, and deliberately not a `let`
-- annotation. It needs no capability VALUE at all — naming the type is
-- enough — so it does not depend on being able to obtain a capability from
-- somewhere, and it therefore survives R2 (see reject/t152) unchanged.
--
-- The `let`-annotation spelling this file originally used relied on the
-- ambient `root_cap`: a console-only module took the root and narrowed it to
-- file-write. R2 closed that route, and with it the reachability of the `let`
-- spelling specifically — a `let` needs a right-hand side, and after R2 every
-- route to a capability value runs through a signature that Check 1 already
-- reads. `bind_ty` is still walked (accept/t145 pins the positive case), but
-- it is defensive now rather than witnessed. This file is what keeps the
-- body-annotation walk under a real test.
--
-- Accept companion: accept/t145.
mod BodyAnnCap do
  needs IO.Console

  fn main() do
    let take = fn (c : Cap(IO.FileWrite)) -> 1
    println("a lambda parameter names a capability this module never declared")
  end
end
